WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:02.050
These two show the harder paths.

2
00:00:02.050 --> 00:00:03.307
In the third example,

3
00:00:03.307 --> 00:00:07.375
the inventory finds a certificate
from an issuer nobody approved.

4
00:00:07.375 --> 00:00:08.765
There's no automatic fix,

5
00:00:08.765 --> 00:00:12.450
because replacing it blindly
could break whoever depends on it.

6
00:00:12.450 --> 00:00:16.360
The PKI owner decides to replace
it through the approved issuer

7
00:00:16.360 --> 00:00:19.975
and block the rogue one, and then
the gated lane does the work.

8
00:00:19.975 --> 00:00:24.738
In the fourth, an old appliance has only
a console, so no machine can reach it.

9
00:00:24.738 --> 00:00:26.448
The owner approves a short,

10
00:00:26.448 --> 00:00:30.413
recorded session with a compensating
control and an end date.

11
00:00:30.413 --> 00:00:33.351
It's visible, approved
first and temporary.

12
00:00:33.351 --> 00:00:36.170
Certificates deserve a
closer look of their own.
